{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 320,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "﻿Modern consumerism has devolved into value \nextraction. Across every industry, quality,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5600,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "service, and choice have all gone down while \nprices go up. Shrinkflation is no longer a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10560,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one-off tactic, but instead a permanent strategy \ningrained across corporate America. It’s not that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15680,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you outgrew your childhood favorites - it’s \nthat they became objectively worse. For the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20160,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "past 15 years, corporations have relentlessly \nhollowed out their brands and products - quietly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25200,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reformulating recipes, simplifying ingredients, \nshrinking portions, and killing off niche items  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30720,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "under the endless pursuit of minimizing cost and \nmaximizing profit. And when confronted, they deny  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36400,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or gaslight, asserting that these changes are \nupgrades even though the public can clearly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40960,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "see that they’re simply paying more for less.\nYet decades ago in the 80s, 90s, and 2000s, these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46960,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "same companies were creative and innovative. They \ndreamed big - launching new brands, products, and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52240,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "formats beyond to win customers on merit as much \nas emotion. They took pride in what they invented.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57680,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today, all that ambition has been liquidated in \nfavor of ruthless short-term profit engineering.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62960,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Cost-cutting is nothing new, but everything \nchanged in 2011 with the release of Moneyball. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68480,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "film was more than just an underdog sports story. \nWhat the Social Network was for nerds, Moneyball  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73600,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was for suits. The movie spread through business \nschools and company HQs. It elevated efficiency  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78560,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from a back-office chore into a corporate \nmandate, redefined innovation as optimization,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 83600,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and popularized the notion that doing things the \nway they’ve always been done is inherently wrong.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88480,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Success was not about swinging for home runs, \nbut battle singles. Decisions should be made with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93280,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "numbers, not instinct. Like baseball players, \nbrands and products should be treated as just  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97680,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "names on a roster. The moment they no longer \nget on base, they must be replaced by whatever  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102400,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the data deems to be more efficient. Ultimately, \nwinning was presented as a math problem - not a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107440,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "creative one. Anyone still swinging for the fences \nwas a dinosaur standing in the way of progress.  \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112560,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This gospel reached boardrooms, consultants, \nexecutives, and Wall Street. No one wanted to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117520,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be the blind, stubborn, scout, much less invest in \nthem. Everyone wanted to be Jonah Hill, Brad Pitt,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123440,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the overachieving, lean, data-driven Oakland \nA’s. No industry weaponized this ethos more than  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129520,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "consumer goods, who had been long looking for an \nexcuse to trade away the never-ending pressure of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134480,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "innovation for the safe, predictable, and clinical \nscience of value extraction. 15 years later,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140880,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these companies still proudly embrace Moneyball, \nholding conferences with Billy Beane, talking up  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145760,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "data, and boasting about proprietary algorithms \n- even as their products get worse and customers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150880,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tune out. For them, profit is no longer the reward \nfor a good product; it is the sole objective and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156240,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only purpose for a product’s existence. In this \nModern MBA episode, we dive into the economics of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161760,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shrinkflation and how this all accelerated \nwith Moneyball in 2010 through the lens of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166640,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the biggest consumer conglomerates in the world.\nThe Moneyball movie laid out 5 tenets - the first  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171840,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was that data should be an absolute authority. \nIntuition is a liability. If the numbers dictate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175200,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an action, no gut feeling, tradition, or \nconventional wisdom should stand in its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179920,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "way. If the data says smaller sizes and higher \nprices result in higher profit, Moneyball says  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185040,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to do it without any hesitation. The second \nwas scarcity as a catalyst for innovation.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190080,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just like how the Oakland A’s were forced to \ninnovate as one of baseball’s poorest teams,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194480,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "organizations were told to run artificially \nlean to drive innovation. Scarcity would create  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 198800,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "urgency, compel teams to ways to do more with \nless, and those efficiencies in turn would become  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 203360,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "robust competitive advantages. The third was to \nbat singles over home runs. Moneyball taught that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208640,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "success is not about flashy breakthroughs, but \nabout simple, steady, incremental improvements.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214080,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Every cut cost and margin improvement, no matter \nhow small, are singles that will accumulate over  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219200,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time into the equivalent of a home-run. \nLet your competitors risk billions trying  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223600,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to catch lighting in a bottle while you safely \nstring small, consistent wins. The fourth was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228800,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "focus. Organizations should focus exclusively on \nproduction, not potential. Any player, product,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234720,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or brand that underperforms is a distraction and a \nliability at-worst that drains critical resources  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238800,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "away from your star assets. And lastly, Moneyball \nmandates to challenge the way things have always  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 244160,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "been done. Conventional wisdom is not to be \ntrusted. The best outcomes are the ones that can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 249200,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be predicted with precision. Numbers strip away \nsubjectivity, provide clarity, and dictate what  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254400,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an organization must do and why. This philosophy \nhas allowed corporations to rebrand their endless  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260000,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cost-cutting and shrinkflation tactics to \nWall Street as some high-tech, visionary,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 264880,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and sophisticated, competitive advantage.\nMoneyball resonated deeply with The Hershey  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269920,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Company, who was perpetually frustrated with \nbeing underdogs in the global candy and snack  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 274480,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "aisle. The Pennsylvania giant dominates the U.S. \nmarket with Reeses, Twizzler’s, Jolly Rancher,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280400,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Hershey’s, but has struggled overseas for \ngenerations. In the 90s, decades before Moneyball,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286160,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the company bet everything on innovation. It \nacquired smaller brands like Milk Duds and PayDay,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291680,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tried to diversify into other categories, and \nrepeatedly launched new products in search of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296240,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the next great chocolate bar. Cookies \u0026 Cream, \nNuggets, Reese’s Sticks, and the Symphony Bar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301360,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were all invented in this era and 3 decades later \nremain permanent members of the Hershey roster.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307040,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Executives prided themselves on vision and \nexecution, stating that every brand launched  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311440,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from within the company since 1990 still remained \nin production. This strategy continued into the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316880,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "2000s with chocolate slices shaped like Pringles, \nReese’s Fast-Break, candy-coated miniature Kisses,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 322560,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Take 5 - the first ever candy to combine \npretzels, caramel, peanut butter, peanuts,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 328000,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and chocolate into a single bar without going \nstale or falling apart. Even classics like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 332720,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Reeses were constantly refreshed with bold \nflavors like Marshmallow, Honey Roasted,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337360,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Banana Cream. But the only way Hershey’s could \nfit all these products on a retailer’s shelves was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342240,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by making most of these items limited-time - there \njust wasn’t enough physical space to carry every  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347120,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "single one. This high-intensity innovation model \nwas meant to grab headlines, excite customers,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 352400,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "keep Hershey’s ahead of their competition. \nIn the mind of their leaders, a new flavor  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356400,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or new product would be more cost-effective \nthan advertising. At the same time, they were  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 360960,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "confident in their R\u0026D. Out of dozens of these \nnew products, it was more than likely that one  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365760,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could become that next billion-dollar brand.  \nBut by 2007, profits had fallen by 60%. Sales  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372480,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "had been growing, but retailers had cut Hershey’s \nshelf space. They were frustrated from constantly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377520,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "having to dump the limited-time products at a \nloss. There were simply too many products and too  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382160,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "little shelf space, so much so that the new brands \nended up cannibalizing the high-margin core brands  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387200,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of Reese’s, Hershey’s and Kisses. Margins fell \nas factories needed to be constantly retooled to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392960,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "support new launches, manufacturing methods, and \ningredients. In 2011, at the peak of Moneyball’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 398880,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "popularity, the board appointed a new CEO who came \nin with the pledge that Hershey would hit the top  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404080,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "end of all its financial targets under his watch. \nUnlike past CEOs who came from marketing or sales,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 409840,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "he was a numbers guy. He publicly promised \nrepeatable success through sophisticated  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 414320,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "statistical models and to put an end to the \ntrial-and-error, spray-and-pray of the past. Under  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 419520,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "JP Bilbrey, Hershey’s turned into a shareholder \nmachine, focused exclusively on efficiency and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424960,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "profit. Within a year, he had dismantled the \nhigh-intensity R\u0026D pipeline, diverted millions of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430400,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dollars into analytics, and famously asserted that \nthe company had spent too long reading yesterday’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 435280,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "news instead of predicting tomorrow’s weather - \njust like the blind old scouts from the movie.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439840,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He proclaimed Hershey’s future as a “knowledge \ncompany”, rather than a candymaker. All budgets  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444960,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were funneled exclusively on 3 star brands - \nReese’s, Hershey’s, and Kit Kat. Any product  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 449920,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not bearing these names was a punitive tax on \nthe company’s best assets. Hundreds of products  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454880,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "discontinued, dozens of brands divested, factories \nshuttered, and hundreds of workers got laid off  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 460480,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "year after year. These cost savings were used to \nfund record dividends and share buybacks. By 2016,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467200,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hershey’s had announced that it would only invest \nin brands that earned their keep. In the CEO’s own  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 472320,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "words, they were no longer going to protect \nbrands simply because of legacy or customer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476960,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "attachment. R\u0026D slowed as the company focused \non batting singles with regular flavor drops  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482240,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and re-moulded shapes for the three star brands. \nWithout innovation, marketing has become the only  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 487440,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lever for growth. Advertising costs have doubled \nas Hershey’s has no other way to manufacture  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492560,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "demand when the products have stopped evolving \nand give customers little to be excited about.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 497200,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "His successor has taken Moneyball to the extreme. \nUnder the pursuit of maximum value extraction,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502560,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the company defines innovation today by pricing \nand packaging, not product. Products like Reese’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 508080,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thins are just ways to get customers to pay a \nhigher price per ounce for less product. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 513200,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "conventional lie-flat bags have been swapped out \nfor stand-up pouches that look bigger and feel  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517840,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fuller on shelves, but in reality, hold less candy \nthan before. The overall lineup remains frozen as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524160,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a few legacy brands like Almond Joy, Mounds, and \nPayday have been kept alive exclusively for the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529920,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "illusion of choice. They’re zombies - they \nget no marketing spend, no R\u0026D budget, and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 535360,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "whatever profits they bring in get harvested for \nshareholders. Modern Hershey’s, for all its data  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 540640,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and high-tech capabilities, is focused on finding \nways to make the product more profitable, rather  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 545840,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than better. By shrinking the roster down to only \nprofitable brands and applying shrinkflation,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 550640,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hershey’s has been able to squeeze revenue growth \nof 3-5% despite selling less chocolate year after  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556320,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "year. The underlying numbers are remarkable - \nprices have been jacked up annually on average  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 561040,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by 6%, sales volume has not meaningfully grown \nin 10 years when you exclude the pandemic,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 566320,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and yet Hershey’s is still making more money \nthan ever. It’s a stark contrast to the 2000s,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 570960,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when the company made every effort to \nkeep prices low in exchange for volume.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 575200,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From the outside, Hershey’s looks like a success. \nExecutives beam at the Moneyball comparison  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580080,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and boast about shrinkflation as some form of \ndata-driven technological advantage. They brag  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585280,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about their record stock growth while shareholders \nreap the generous dividends and analysts applaud  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 590000,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "margins. But look past these numbers and the truth \nis simple - fewer and fewer people are actually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 595360,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "buying the product. Hershey’s today treats its \nbrands as an inelastic product. The harsh truth  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 600560,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is that the company has also completely forgotten \nhow to invent. These days, they would rather buy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605040,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an emerging snack or candy for growth than to \ncreate something original like they did regularly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609680,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the 90s. Fifteen years of cost-cutting and \nnickel-and-diming have stripped the company of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 614320,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the talent, muscle, and understanding needed \nto invent something genuinely new that isn’t  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 619040,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a reboot or extension of an existing product. To \nsidestep cocoa costs, they've acquired trending  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 624400,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "brands like Krave Jerky, BarkThins, and SkinnyPop. \nUnder Moneyball logic, these are undervalued  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629760,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "players that have already proven that they can \nget on-base. By plugging them into Hershey’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634000,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mature distribution and sales machine, the company \ncan safely improve ROI and bypass the gamble of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639600,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "innovation - even if that means spending millions \nmore post-acquisition on celebrity endorsements  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 644720,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and mass advertising. Despite its billions and \nlegacy, the company today is content to let others  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 650160,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "take the swing and then step in only once the home \nrun is hit. In many ways, Hershey’s is less of a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 655600,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "candymaker and more of a bank: capital-efficient, \nrisk-averse, and focused only on value extraction.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661840,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Since 2010, the company has spent over $7 \nbillion on dividends alone - a fraction of that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 667440,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could have easily funded multiple products.\nEvery business is asking the same question:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672960,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how do we make AI work for us? The possibilities \nare endless, and guessing is too risky. Here’s the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678640,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "problem: while you’re trying to figure it out, \nthe competition is already moving. And every  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 682880,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "day you sit on the sidelines, you’re losing \nground. Forget the buzzwords and 'snake oil'  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 687440,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pitches. With NetSuite, you get real, actionable \nAI that actually gets your business - no fluff,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 693360,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "big zany words, just results you \ncan use literally tomorrow.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696880,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "NetSuite is the #1 AI Cloud ERP, trusted by \nover 43,000 businesses. NetSuite is a unified  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 703760,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "suite that brings your financials, inventory, \ncommerce, HR, and CRM into a single source of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 709280,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "truth. Even great instincts only get better with \ndata. By connecting all the data in NetSuite,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 714160,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your AI gets smarter. It doesn’t just guess - it \nknows. NetSuite intelligently automates routine  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719440,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tasks so you can deliver real value, cut costs and \nmake fast AI-powered decisions with confidence and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 724960,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "total flexibility. Now with NetSuite AI Connector, \nyou can use your favorite AI to connect to your  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730560,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "actual business data and ask every question you’ve \never dreamed of - from key customers and cash flow  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735760,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to inventory trends and accounting projections, \nplus it’ll automate all your tiresome manual  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740640,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "processes. Let’s see your competition do THAT. \nNetSuite isn’t another ChatGPT wrapper, it’s AI  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 746400,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "built into the system that runs your business.   \nOur goal is to grow to seven-figures and once  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752080,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Modern MBA reaches that milestone, we plan to \nadopt Netsuite. We sit on a mountain of data  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756480,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but production is so intense that no one has \nthe bandwidth to analyze the business. We’re  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 760960,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "constantly balancing creativity with economics, \nbut it’s hard to derive without pulling ourselves  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 765760,
    "dDurationMs": 1238,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "away from production. Whether your company earns \nmillions or even hundreds of millions, NetSuite  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 766998,
    "dDurationMs": 1882,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "helps you stay ahead of the pack.        \nIf your revenues are at least in the seven  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 768880,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "figures, get the business guide Demystifying \nAI, free, at Netsuite dot com slash MODERNMBA.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 775280,
    "dDurationMs": 10160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "THe guide is free to you at NetSuite dot com slash \nMODERNMBA. NetSuite dot com slash MODERNMBA.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 785440,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Kellogg’s has followed the same trajectory. In \nthe 1990s and 2000s, Kellogg was under siege by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 791200,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "private labels, which were aggressively cloning \ntheir flagship cereals at half the price. Kellogg  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 796320,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "still promised investors growth, dividends, and \nshare buybacks—but believed firmly that product  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 801200,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "innovation was the engine that would deliver these \nresults. Since they could never win on price,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 806160,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they focused on creating breakthrough products \nthat were so creative and technical that they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 810240,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could never be replicated. They prioritized volume \nover margin, deliberately kept price increases  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 815440,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "below inflation, and challenged themselves to \nfind ways to get customers to eat more Kellogg  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 820320,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "products. Like Hershey, these decades were periods \nof intense innovation - the Rice Krispy Treat and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 825920,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Eggo Cereals were feats of food engineering that \nblew kids minds around the country, Special K was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 831520,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the first ever cereal with freeze-dried fruit that \nwouldn’t stain the milk, Cereal Straws lined with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 836560,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fruit cream, and the magical chocolate-filled \ncenter of Krave. As consumers began eating less  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 841760,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cereal, Kellogg's tried to adapt with snack bars, \ngranola, Pop Tarts, and frozen sandwiches.  \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 847440,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, these innovations could not reverse \nthe overall category decline. Kellogg was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852320,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "trapped in a dying market and caught between \nrising corn costs and falling sales volumes.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 857040,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Profits slipped and the stock stagnated. \nIn 2011, just months after Moneyball,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 861840,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the board appointed an accountant to do what \nJonah Hill had done in the movie - a numbers guy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 866720,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rather than a cereal man, who could detach from \nlegacy thinking, cut ruthlessly, and steady the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 871760,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ship. Just like in Hershey’s, this new Moneyball \nCEO deemed innovation as wasteful. Products that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 877440,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "required multi-step manufacturing and complex \nexperimentation - the very moats that Kellogg’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 882240,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "had built to fight private label knockoffs - \nwere discontinued. Factories were shuttered,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 887120,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "production was consolidated, workers were laid \noff, and the R\u0026D budget was slashed over the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 891360,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "course of the next decade. All these cost savings \nare used to fund dividends and share buybacks and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 896400,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "whatever money leftover from that got reinvested \nin a narrow group of high-performing brands. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 901680,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Kellogg’s strategy today is just to squeeze as \nmuch profit as possible from shrinking sales  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 906320,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through annual price hikes and shrinkflation. \nThey trimmed the depth of every cereal box while  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 910800,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "keeping the front the same, so customers won’t \nnotice they’re paying more for less. Pringles  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 915440,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cans shrank and the chips themselves got smaller, \nthinner, and lighter. Rice Krispie Treats, which  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 920080,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "used to be sold in big sheets, are now mostly sold \nin tiny, individually-wrapped squares. Products  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 925280,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that underperform are quickly discontinued and new \nreleases are generally just repackaged variations  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 930800,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or extensions of core brands. Like Hershey, \nKellogg has forgotten how to invent. Without  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 935840,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that creative muscle or core growth, their \nonly option today is to buy home runs - which  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 940480,
    "dDurationMs": 3258,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they did in 2017 with RXBAR for $600M. Despite 15 \nconsecutive years of volume decline, Wall Street  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 943738,
    "dDurationMs": 3542,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is still somehow sold on their Moneyball promise. \nKellogg’s has regressed from a category-defining  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 947280,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "giant into a soulless margin machine, focused \nonly on finding ways to charge more and hand out  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 952720,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "less of the same 50-year-old cornflake.  \nIn the 90s, there was also General Mills,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 957680,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who ruled school playgrounds and lunchboxes with \ninventions like Fruit Roll-Ups, Dunkaroos, and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 962880,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gushers. The company prided itself on innovation \n- having transformed yogurt into a portable snack  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 968480,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with Go-Gurt, kickstarted the dessert cereal \ncraze with Reese’s Puffs, and turned fiber  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 973360,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into a daily indulgence with Fiber One. But in \nthe mid-2010s, Moneyball had become the dominant  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 979200,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "religion in corporate America. While Kellogg’s, \nHershey, and Kraft Heinz were cutting costs under  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 984640,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the guise of becoming leaner, more profitable, \nhigh-tech, data-driven companies, General Mills  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 990240,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "looked like it was stuck in the past. Its pursuit \nof home runs, intuition-based decision making,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 995360,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bloated R\u0026D spend, and prioritization \nof volume over margin made Wall Street  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1000320,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nervous. So when the company missed earnings in \n2014, the response was swift. Under pressure,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1006400,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "General Mill’s executives embraced Moneyball. \nThey stopped trying to invent the next big thing,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1011280,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "invested millions into analytics, and tunnel \nvisioned on batting singles. The small group of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1016480,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "brands that generated 75% of revenue were rewarded \nwith investment. Everything else got purged.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1022320,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Factories were closed, workers laid off, regional \nbrands eliminated, and all these savings were once  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1027840,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "again recycled into dividends, share buybacks, \nand extensions of the same core billion-dollar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1033360,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "brands. Innovation fell as General Mills went \nfrom hundreds of new products a year to just less  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1038640,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than 50 through the decade. Legacy brands that \ndefined entire grocery aisles like Progresso,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1043840,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Betty Crocker, and Pillsbury, were placed on \nmaintenance mode. Innovation and promotion  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1048480,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were no longer permitted and their declining sales \nwere accepted as long as their margins improved.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1053520,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Coupons were also systematically gutted.  \nUnder Moneyball, it’s better to sell one can of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1058320,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "soup at ten cents of profit than ten cans at one \ncent each. The company’s pride and joy today is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1063920,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not in its products or customer trust, but instead \nin data science. They boast that their predictive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1069200,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "algorithms can calculate the exact cent that a \nprice can be raised or the ounces of cereal can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1073920,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be shrunk from a box of Honey Nut Cheerios before \ncustomers will stop buying. Their latest product  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1079200,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is an embodiment of this same approach. It’s \nYoplait packaged into tiny glass jars, branded in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1084720,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "French, and marketed as high-end and it’s been a \nsuccess at getting customers to pay more for less  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1089920,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the same thing. Today, General Mills has staked \nits future on pet food for no other reason beyond  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1095440,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "numbers. It’s a higher-margin, faster-growing \ncategory when stacked against cereal, yogurt,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1100640,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and breakfast and under Moneyball logic, the \nright decision to make. Like Hershey and Kellogg’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1106000,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "before, General Mills has pivoted to buying growth \nafter realizing that they can’t cut their way to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1110960,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it. Since they don’t understand how to get on base \nin the first place, they can only follow the data,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1116000,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "jump on trends after they materialize, \nand pray that demand lasts long enough  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1120000,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for them to recoup their acquisition costs.\nWhile Kellogg’s and General Mills adopted  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1124320,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Moneyball under Wall Street pressure, Kraft \nHeinz - under the iron grip of its private  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1128960,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equity owners - pushed Moneyball so far that \nthe company broke. What was once a powerhouse  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1133920,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of America’s most legendary brands - Heinz \nketchup, Philadelphia Cream Cheese, Kraft Singles,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1139680,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Oscar Mayer hot dogs - today is a zombie \nwith no soul or purpose. In the 90s and 2000s,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1146240,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Kraft and Heinz were titans of the American \npantry. Brand value was everything. They saw  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1151200,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "brands as emotional and functional assets that \nrequired careful nurturing. While they still  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1156000,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "promised dividends and earnings to Wall Street, \nthey invested in product superiority and minimized  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1160960,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "price hikes so Americans would naturally \nprioritize their brands over private-label  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1165360,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "generics. They created proprietary macaroni \nnoodles for Kraft Mac and Cheese, engineered  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1170480,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the first-ever, self-rising, frozen pizza for \nDigiorno’s, pioneered the upside-down bottle for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1175440,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "easier application of Heinz ketchup, and invented \nshelf-stable, portable Jell-O pudding cups.  \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1180640,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The inflection point came in 2013, when private \nequity took over. Inspired by Moneyball,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1186240,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they saw Heinz and Kraft’s sprawling factories, \nmassive R\u0026D campuses, bloated marketing budgets,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1191760,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and hordes of workers as waste. In the context \nof a spreadsheet, this constant trial-and-error  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1196720,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "innovation was completely unnecessary. If 9 out \nof every 10 customers were going to buy Kraft  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1201760,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Mac \u0026 Cheese, why leave millions on the table \ncreating new pasta shapes, launching new brands,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1206880,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and worrying about price hikes? With Moneyball, \nthere was finally a legitimate reason to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1211440,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ignore tradition, push the boundary on price, \nand optimize for profit. With data and analytics,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1217120,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "private equity believed they could unlock \nefficiencies and achieve returns well beyond  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1221280,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the 2% annual growth and 10% margins the old \nguard had been content with. By trimming the fat  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1227120,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and replacing instinct with algorithms, Moneyball \nwould quickly unlock billions in hidden value.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1232400,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They implemented zero-based budgeting across the \nwhole company, forcing workers to justify every  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1237360,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "single dollar from scratch under the conviction \nthat this would unearth inefficiencies and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1241760,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "underperformers. The only growth that now counted \nwas profitable growth. Innovation shifted away  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1247280,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from product and towards packaging and pricing. \nMarket share was irrelevant unless it generated  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1252320,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pure profit. Brands, initiatives, factories, and \ndivisions that failed to pass this math test were  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1257600,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shuttered. The Kraft Heinz of the 2010s lived \nby the mantra that it was better to sell nothing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1261360,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rather than sell for less. Every penny saved was \na penny worth earned. Heinz ketchup bottles shrank  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1266880,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from 20 ounces to 19. Single-use packets went from \n10 ml to 7 ml. To consumers, the difference of a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1274240,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "few drops went unnoticed, but to the company, it’s \n30% more profit. Every box of Kraft Mac \u0026 Cheese  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1279920,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was reduced by 25 grams, but the noodles \nthemselves were made thinner and smaller,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1284480,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so the box would still sound full when shaken. \nOscar Mayer bacon was repackaged to 12 ounces  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1290160,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and rebranded as “center-cut” so the company \ncould sell 25% less meat for the same price as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1295120,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a full pound. Lunchables were reformulated \nwith thinner, cheaper, processed meats and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1299920,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cheeses. The cost-cutting was so aggressive \nthat the product was eventually kicked out of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1303840,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the National School Lunch Program for failing \nto meet the nutritional standards. All these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1308320,
    "dDurationMs": 9200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tactics fueled record dividends and profits.\nBut by 2019, Moneyball had hit its wall. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1317520,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "math failed as customers simply walked away. Kraft \nHeinz took a $15 billion loss on Kraft and Oscar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1323360,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Mayer, admitting that they had harvested all \nthe value and trust that these brands had taken  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1327680,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "generations to painstakingly accumulate. With no \nfat left to cut, its leaders have started cutting  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1332800,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bone. The company has lurched from trend \nto trend—vegan mayo, plant-based burgers,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1338080,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "kids’ flavored water, and now pet food. THeir \nleaders don’t have any personal conviction in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1342400,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these futures, instead, they’re just following \nwhat the data says. Planters, once a crown jewel  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1347360,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the company portfolio and undisputed king of \nthe nut aisle, was sold off under the explanation  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1352400,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of market pressure and rising costs. But in \nreality, the company opted for an easy exit  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1357520,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because they had forgotten not just how to \ninvent, but also how to compete. To compete  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1361760,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "requires effort, investment, and talent - the very \nthings that Kraft Heinz had systematically purged  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1367120,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from itself in the name of efficiency.  \nNestlé, Unilever, and P\u0026G have all suffered  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1372400,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same fate. These giants created some of \nthe most iconic products in the 90s and 2000s:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1377760,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "slow-churned Dreyer’s that made half-fat ice cream \ntaste like full-fat, chocolate Wonder Balls that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1382960,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hid surprise toys, and gummy-crunchy \nNerds Ropes. This creativity extended  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1387680,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "well into other aisles like Axe body spray, \nsingle-use Swiffers that replaced messy mops,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1392960,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Tide Pods that eliminated the hassle of \nliquid detergent. It’s no coincidence that these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1398160,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "companies began their pivot in the years following \nthe film's release. While Moneyball might not have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1402800,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "been the literal trigger, it popularized and \nlegitimized optimization as innovation. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1407920,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Cost cutting used to be seen as a weapon of \nlast resort for failing companies and desperate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1412320,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "leaders. It’s an easy lever anyone can pull. Even \nwhen performed with algorithms or guided by data,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1418320,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cost-cutting is still cost-cutting. It should not \nbe seen as some sophisticated, modern, high-tech  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1423040,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strategy or as a substitute for vision. In the \n90s and 2000s, all these corporations - Kellogg’s,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1429040,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hershey’s, Kraft Heinz, and General Mills were \nunder the same pressure for earnings, but their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1433680,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "leaders chose to take a long-term, holistic \nview to decisions. Yet today, under Moneyball,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1438720,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the pendulum has swung: those who optimize \nfor short-term targets even at the expense  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1443120,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of long-term growth, are the ones who rise up the \nladder. The result is the corporate monoculture  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1447920,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "today where executives who see only spreadsheets, \nnot humans, boards that appoint number-crunchers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1453040,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over risk-takers, and organizations built to \nachieve yesterday’s targets yet entirely incapable  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1458160,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of inventing the future. When you let the numbers \ndictate everything, you become reactive and can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1462960,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only see what’s in front of you. In baseball, \nMoneyball works in the regular season—but  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1467760,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fails in the playoffs. Similarly, corporations \ntoday are buying short-term Wall Street wins,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1473040,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but sacrificing the long-term consumer trust \nthat drove their success in the first place."
    } ]
  } ]
}
